---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="WCAG data sources"
   summary="a11ied keeps its standards knowledge local, versioned, and boring on purpose. That is a feature. If the agent cannot explain where a criterion, technique, or strategy came from, it should not be making claims with confidence."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>What ships in the repo</h2>
      <p>
         The source of truth is <code>packages/wcag-data</code>. The sync step pulls structured WCAG,
         ACT mapping, Quickref technique, and axe rule data, then turns it into generated artifacts the
         rest of the product can read without scraping prose pages on demand.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Upstream sources that matter</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>W3C WCAG data for criteria, levels, and normative text</li>
         <li>W3C ACT mapping for machine-readable rule-to-criterion coverage</li>
         <li>W3C Quickref technique and failure data</li>
         <li>Local <code>axe-core</code> rule metadata for automated coverage joins</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What the operator should do first</h2>
      <ol>
         <li>Resolve the requested WCAG version. The default is 2.2 unless the task says otherwise.</li>
         <li>Resolve the criterion or target level before opening a browser.</li>
         <li>Check coverage and verification strategy before promising automation.</li>
      </ol>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>What this does not mean</h2>
      <p>
         Having local WCAG data does not mean the tool can prove every criterion automatically. Some
         rows map cleanly to axe. Some need Guidepup patterns. Some still need a human to look at the
         thing and make a judgment call.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
